one of the biggest impediments to  getting things done  in the united states senate when you  have a majority  but not a 60-seat majority is to  filibuster  and the filibuster is a mechanism  through which even if you  only need 50 votes to pass something  you need 60 votes to end debate  on that very issue and move  to the vote and this is a mechanism that  allows a party  in the minority to indefinitely hold up  voting on legislation  and to obstruct and to do all of the  things that republicans led by mitch  mcconnell  love to do to democrats now democrats  could blow up the filibuster completely  this is called the nuclear option and  we've talked about it before  but it is a double-edged sword and it  could as i've  talked to you before about it could come  back to really bite democrats when  republicans are back  in the majority at some point in the  future this has been a point of  contention about which there are  reasonable positions on both sides  but in an interview with abc news that  aired last night  joe biden told george stephanopoulos he  does  support reforming the senate filibuster  not eliminating it with the nuclear  option but reforming it well what  exactly is the reform that joe biden  is interested in let's take a look at  the clip zach so aren't you going to  have to choose  i know you've been reluctant to do away  with the filibuster aren't you gonna  have to choose  between preserving the filibuster and  advancing your agenda  yes but here's the choice i don't think  you have to eliminate the filibuster you  have to  do it what it used to be when i first  got to the senate back in the old days  when you used to be around there and  that is that a filibuster you had to  stand up and command the floor  and you had to keep talking alone you  couldn't call for you know  they no no one could say you know quorum  call  once you stopped talking you lost that  and someone could move in and say  i moved the question of so you got to  work for the filibuster so you're for  that reform you're for bringing back the  talking filibuster i am  that's what it was supposed to be look i  think don't hold me to the numbers  george but i think between 1960 and 2000  they were like i'm making this number up  i don't know  there were like uh you know 50  filibusters and now they're like 200  since then  since then just put a hold on it that's  it yeah i mean you know so  the idea it almost is getting to the  point where  there's you know democracies having a  hard time functioning  a hard time functioning now this is  notable for a few reasons now just a  couple of days ago  joe biden's press secretary jen saki was  asked about the filibuster during a  press briefing  and she said joe biden does not want to  change any rules on the filibuster  so that seems to have changed but more  importantly on substance  biden doesn't say get rid of the  filibuster  just make it more difficult by requiring  an actual talking filibuster and  this is an important detail to  understand right now  senators can indefinitely extend debate  on a topic  and prevent voting on that topic by  asking  to extend debate period and just never  allowing debate to end when someone  moves to end debate and vote  republicans can say no i want to extend  debate and they're able to do it  in the past senators actually had to  hold the floor  and speak and keep speaking to delay  a bill and the idea that joe biden is  suggesting  and others have also mentioned is to  require the actual  speaking to take place so senators can't  just  ask for an extension and then another  one and then another one  they actually have to talk through the  filibuster and this would have two  effects  first of all it would require  republicans to actually  organize to have back to back-to-back  speakers in the senate  never-ending with their 50 senators they  actually  would be able to do it but how long will  they be willing to do it from a  practical standpoint  24-7 day after day after day  but maybe more importantly a talking  filibuster  that goes on and on and on merely to  prevent a vote  on an issue is going to pick up media  attention the longer it goes  and presumably it will come off as  exactly the type of blatant obstruction  that it is  now the possible political  miscalculation  is that we're assuming the current  republican party  would be mad about that and they would  say this must end let's just have a vote  but the current republican party has  been so propagandized  that they might not actually be that mad  about an endless talking filibuster they  might like it  but the gamble is that the left and the  centrists and maybe some republicans  would say guys we  we don't have control of the senate this  isn't really democratic  this has to end you have to put the  issue to a vote maybe that is what  what would happen i don't know now why  does this matter bigger picture  it matters because filibuster rules  in the next two years might actually  determine  does washington d.c become a state does  puerto rico become a state  does automatic voter registration get  passed  and a whole bunch of other important  issues this is not trivial and the point  is that right now  it's too easy to filibuster with very  little work and without much political  cost  now there's a couple of other ideas that  have been floated one i this this is not  what biden supports but these are other  ideas  to affect the filibuster without ending  it  one idea is you have to have 40  senators present to extend the  filibuster right now it's if you  don't have 60 you can't end debate  the idea is to flip the onus and put it  on republicans to keep having 40 of  their 50 senators  in the chamber 24 7 to continue  extending the filibuster  that would be a very good idea without  blowing it up  the other thing is that the talking  filibuster  it should have to be relevant to the  topic at hand in other words  if you are trying to end debate on  automatic voter registration  and go to a vote you can't get up there  and read a dr seuss book to filibuster  you should be filibustering materially  with something connected to automatic  voter registration  and that's another possibility that  could be done we will see  this is a really big deal and uh you  know it's one of these things where  a lot of this stuff like oh joe when is  joe biden going to do his first  press conference sort of like you know  it's an inside  the beltway story that also has very  little  impact on americans the filibuster is  something  a lot of people are not following and  aren't aware of  but it actually does relate to things  that would have a very direct and  serious impact  uh on americans in terms of what it  would allow the senate to do so we're  going to continue following now  this is this is joe biden's latest view  on this issue let's now talk about  the flip side of the coin which is mitch  mcconnell's view  on the issue one of our sponsors is  monk pack it's tough to get excited  about most granola bars they have a lot  of carbs  they don't taste that great they don't  really satisfy your cravings  but monk pack has something totally  different monk pack  keto granola bars are sweet and  delicious  with a soft and chewy texture  only 140 calories one gram of sugar  and only two to three net carbs which  make them work  perfectly for the keto or low carb  lifestyles  they come in excellent flavors like  coconut cocoa chip  honey nut blueberry almond vanilla  that's my new favorite  i love these because you can just grab  them anytime maybe it's a quick  breakfast an afternoon snack when you're  busy  forget the dry bland granola bars you've  had in the past  try out monk pack keto granola bars  if you don't love them like i do they'll  give you all of your money back  you'll get 20 off when you go to  monkpack.com and use the coupon code  pacman the link is underneath this video
 

